{"name":"Coral Atoll","hex":"#dc938d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many species of marine invertebrates in the class Anthozoa, most of which build hard calcium carbonate skeletons and form colonies, or a colony belonging to one of those species.","A hard substance made of the skeletons of these organisms."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of coral.","Having the orange-pink color of coral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coral"},{"term":"atoll","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of island consisting of a ribbon reef that nearly or entirely surrounds a lagoon and supports, in most cases, one to many islets on the reef platform."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atoll"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atoll","blocks":[{"text":"An atoll is a ring-shaped island, including a coral rim that encircles a lagoon. There may be coral islands or cays on the rim. Atolls are located in warm tropical or subtropical parts of the oceans and seas where corals can develop. Most of the approximately 440 atolls in the world are in the Pacific Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Tetiaroa_from_sky.JPG/330px-Tetiaroa_from_sky.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atoll"},{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral"},{"title":"Coral (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The various tones of the color coral are orange, red and pink representations of the colors of those cnidarians known as precious corals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/500px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/1280px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/500px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map from Charles Darwin's 1842 The Structure and Distribution of Coral Reefs showing the world's major groups of atolls and coral reefs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOn_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/250px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/500px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/250px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APenrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.